e are two T3s. But the unshown tiles list T3 as 3, so there are three T3s left. So maybe I have two T3s, one in the meld and one in the hand. That's possible. So the hand tiles are the ones not in the melds. So the five tiles are W4, W7, T3, T7, B5. So I need to play one of these.\n\nThe valid actions are Play W7, Play T3, Play B5, Play W4, Play T7. So I need to choose which tile to discard. The goal is to maximize my score or set up for a good hand. Let's look at the current melds and hand. The melds are all chows in the T and B suits. The hand has W4, W7, T3, T7, B5. The B5 is part of the [B3, B4, B5] meld, but if it's in the hand, maybe that's a duplicate? Wait, no. The meld [B3, B4, B5] uses B3, B4, B5. So the B5 in the hand is a fourth one? But the unshown tiles list B5 as 3, meaning there are three left. So if I have one B5 in hand, and the meld uses one, that's two, so there are two B5s in my hand? Wait, no. The melds are part of the hand. The total tiles in the melds are 3 chows (each 3 tiles) = 9, plus the five in hand = 14. So each tile in the melds is part of the hand. So the B5 in the hand is part of the meld [B3, B4, B5], meaning I have one B5 in the meld. But the hand lists B5 as 1, so maybe that's a mistake. This is confusing. Let me try to parse it again.\n\nThe melds are three chows: [T3, T4, T5], [B3, B4, B5], [T4, T5, T6]. So each of these uses three tiles. The hand tiles are [W4, W7, T3, T7, B5]. So the T3 and B5 in the hand are part of the melds? That doesn't make sense. Unless the hand tiles are the ones not in the melds. Wait, no. The melds are part of the hand. So the total tiles in the hand are 14. The three chows take 9 tiles, so the remaining five are the hand tiles. So the hand tiles are the ones not in the melds. Therefore, the T3 and B5 in the hand are separate from the ones in the melds. But that would mean I have two T3s (one in the meld and one in the hand) and two B5s (one in the meld and one in the hand). However, the unshown tiles